the next program i would like to talk  about is called bioinformatics for  infectious diseases and this is the  application of  biomedical  data analysis to study  outbreaks for example of epidemics like  we saw in this pandemic but also how to  connect it to  genomic detail in the pathogen and an  understanding of how the pathogen enters  the host and what is the response of the  immune system  so again raquel to your question about  being able to access this recording  after this webinar you will receive an  email with the recorded version or you  can go and and look uh pine biotech look  us up on youtube and the recording is  going to be there on our channel  so um in the context of bioinformatics  for infectious diseases we will look at  a few different specific methods right  first of all we will look at sequence  variation and alignment how we can use  alignment to each other to understand  differences of genomic sequences between  themselves or how do we organize them  into groups using evolutionary and  phylogenetic analysis we will also look  at discovery of variance and association  between variants and specific changes  for example at the protein level or at  the population level and then finally  we'll touch on transcriptomic analysis  of host response primarily around  infected uninfected mock infected types  of experiments where we can understand  the host responses at the cellular and  tissue  level  so uh the beginning of the program we'll  start by kind of comparing and  contrasting infectious diseases with  non-infectious diseases we will then  talk about different pathogens bacteria  viruses  parasites and we'll talk about how they  cause disease what is it that we  actually call infectious disease how  does it manifest what are the specific  things that we look at and how can we  analyze those with different types of  molecular uh indicators and so that will  allow us to understand  uh  using again some case studies how  a number of different types of  could be used to characterize disease  and understand disease progression as  well as outcomes  and that will be uh kind of the main  idea of this program is it's multi-omics  data in the context of infectious  diseases  so  the um  uh  i i think uh um the idea here has become  you know infectious diseases have become  one of those examples where these  advanced new technologies like next  generation sequencing have been used  extensively to almost in real time track  the development of an outbreak of an  infectious disease and also characterize  in very great detail how the  the pathogen interacts with the host in  order to  address that problem and create a  diagnostic  create a panel or a simple process that  could identify a very specific strain of  a pathogen that we now know better how  to deal with  also it has been used to identify uh the  types of vaccines for example that we  would like to design or an intervention  like an antiviral or a drug that will  interfere with that process of infection  and so using these kinds of case studies  we're going to take a look at how does  that translate to actually understanding  the methodology what do we do with data  what kinds of data and how do we  actually practically make use of that  data to arrive at such conclusions  so again i talked a little bit about the  different types of methods that we will  cover and importantly it will come with  several different examples project  examples that you can try  cyrus cov2 genomics of course ebola  virus epidemic as well as malaria  infection and again this will be  primarily around genomic and  transcriptomic data  for hana i think parisa here has a  question on how to register in ilab so  if you could please share that with her  so finally i want to talk about a  program that is not specific to a type  of data or a type of an example but it's  more focused on the project that you  would like to complete during this time  and this is applicable to two types of  people so number one researchers whether  you are an undergraduate high school  student or faculty  if your goal is to perform research and  you would like to  find a specific data set  analyze that data and come out with a  poster presentation or a publication  this is what this program is going to be  designed around  and the second example of someone is  maybe someone who would like to  understand the process of research so  one you know what kind of research you'd  like to do you have a goal and you know  how to more or less get there you need  to help with bioinformatics or the  second one you  don't know what research looks like and  you would like to understand how to  perform research but using  bioinformatics because maybe you don't  have access to a wet lab  or maybe you  are just trying to maybe do two things  at the same time  so the research fellowship is really  around the process of  kind of determining what your research  question is  finding a data set that can help you  address your research question and then  being able to  address  you know the kind of  question that you have with the evidence  identified in a particular data set  and the enablement that we offer here is  big data analysis infrastructure so we  will support your big data analysis  needs if you need to analyze the data  set with 10 000 samples and you don't  you know you can't do that on your  computer you will be able to do it here  mentorship so someone will work with you  to identify a question refine that  question make sure that that question um  could be answered with the data that  you've selected and then  help you get there  and along the way we'll provide you with  opportunities to train yourself to be  able to do this independently so instead  of this being training first training is  embedded into that process because  ultimately if you don't know how to  perform a particular type of analysis  you can just ask someone to do it for  you or here you will learn how to do it  on your own and be able to apply it to  that project  and we've selected a  number of directions on how you can  actually do this in the context of  precision medicine population health  biotechnology and agriculture or space  and astrobiology  so the first step here is going to start  with an overview of the general  background  how to do literature review identify  different things that people have  already done to select something that is  still unanswered or maybe there's a gap  in research or maybe a debate and  controversy and how to use that process  of literature review to be able to  identify a very specific question that  you would like to focus on  once we do that then we have a very  well-defined process that will include  weekly meetings  submitting a form about your progress  keeping a journal of your  progress from learning to identifying a  research project to then conducting the  analysis  and eventually  working with the community of both  mentors and other participants to go  through that whole process and submit  the project and the project will first  of all be submitted on the portal so on  the portal  after you complete your profile  and uh you you know that is free you can  just complete your profile  and update it  you will then be able to uh  start working on a project after you  have 10 000 points and so that's where  the training comes in so you still in  the context of this program will have to  go through some training and importantly  we have another project that is designed  to guide you through the different steps  of literature review identifying data  sets on public repositories like ncbi  and then working through your analysis  once you have done that you will be able  to fill in sections of a project from  title to abstract to methodology etc and  along the way get guidance from our  mentors that will help refine and kind  of accelerate that process for you and  we have a number of uh individuals that  have completed the resea